WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.590
Film Courage: Jason, from an actor’s perspective
what are you looking at in terms of old footage,

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.820
what are you studying?

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.360
Jason Clarke: Everything, everything I can
get my hands on.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.570
One of the best things I got my hands on was
this old sailing footage of Ted and Joan,

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.590
sailing the white waters of the boats that
they sailed on at this location.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:25.160
Just seeing them that day on the phone in
the gear, young dudes, Ray-Bans, Joe was smoking

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.699
a cigarette, they were sailing the boat.

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:30.144
You’re looking for something that captures
them when they…you’re looking for both,

00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:31.210
the public ones, the unguarded ones.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.210
The little moments.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:33.210
The mannerisms.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.220
You know, the funeral.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.600
There is so much on these guys, the whole
family.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.410
I’ve watched so much on these guys.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.030
I’ve watched a lot and read a lot on the
other brothers, not just Ted.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.810
I didn’t just go for Ted, I went for the
others.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.110
I went for the family.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:51.320
I went for pictures of Joe, how to behave
around your father.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.480
There is so much there.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.480
And particularly the speeches.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.809
There are some of the greatest speeches ever
said, ever written.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:00.050
Particularly by Bobby.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.330
Film Courage: When did you know you wanted
to be an actor?

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.325
Jason Clarke: When did I know I wanted to
be an actor?

00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:08.920
I think when I decided I’d rather sit home
and watch movies than go to University.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.890
“There is something in this!”

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.689
And I really wanted to be it when I couldn’t…You
realize you really want to be actor when you

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:18.779
can’t quite get there on auditions.

00:01:18.779 --> 00:01:20.389
You’re not quite getting a job, you just
want it.

00:01:20.389 --> 00:01:21.389
You can feel it.

00:01:21.389 --> 00:01:23.969
I want to get in that door.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:27.439
Film Courage: We hear about the character’s
arc.

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:31.250
So when you are about to go into production,
how much are you thinking of that, and what

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.250
was Ted’s arc?

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.250
Jason Clarke: A lot.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.749
Ted’s [arc] was huge.

00:01:35.749 --> 00:01:39.420
You’re thinking about that a lot, of course.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.729
The best (obviously) is when you shoot in
sequence (you go from one to the other).

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:44.729
But you never do that.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:49.070
So I’m having to make sure that if I’m
ending here, I’m starting there.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.739
You try to give it the most that you can so
you’re not just repeating it.

00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:52.989
So you’re really plotting it.

00:01:52.989 --> 00:01:55.869
John [Director John Curran] and I did a lot
of work on that, going over the other scenes.

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:01.310
Not just rehearsing the scenes, talking over
them, rewording them, discussing them.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.539
Ted has a massive one.

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:06.639
He starts off and he goes through literally
a sausage maker.

00:02:06.639 --> 00:02:13.189
I mean he’s ground down to come out to that
final thing where 3-2-1, the pressure’s

00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:15.790
on and you forget that that was a live event.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.939
He got handed a bunch of really bad cards.

00:02:18.939 --> 00:02:22.890
There’s no 15-second pause, it’s on.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.700
Here it is, your whole career, your whole
life, your family, your moral judgment, everything…it’s

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.700
down to this.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.239
Sitting in a room propped up on books [referring
to the press conference].

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:40.469
Ted’s journey of his own moral choice and
the choices that he made A) to walk away from

00:02:40.469 --> 00:02:46.689
the car, to not stop at Dike House and knock
on the door or make a phone call at the bridge,

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:51.239
it’s a full on journey of how he justified
this to himself and worked it out.

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:54.420
Or how he decided he wanted to live or whatever
he was going to come around to.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.310
I mean even on the phone when he goes to the
police station, he called the mother because

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.501
he does know it’s the right thing to do.

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:02.980
To call her up and tell her rather than somebody
else.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.510
So there’s just so much turmoil, up and
down, before we get to the very end.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.180
You know, and then I think that’s also reflected
in society.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.989
How does society process this like it does
and continue to vote him in?

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:21.989
Film Courage: Right, he still had to keep
this public face up, which is so brilliantly

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:25.849
portrayed by you where he walks down the streets
and he’s ruminating about something.

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:29.560
Jason Clarke: Yeah…well I mean he was Ted,
he wasn’t used to this.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.520
Is anybody ready for the fact that you’ve
gone away from a party, you’re having a

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.310
good night, it’s been a year since your
brother’s gone in front of everybody.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.140
You’re catching up with some people that
worked passionately on this thing, you’ve

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:46.159
set a sailing boat, you’re relaxed coming
from Washington (D.C.) and all the sudden

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.159
bang!

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:50.079
You’re upside down on the bottom of a pond.

00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:52.920
Coming out…and then where’s the girl?

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.780
And then it’s dark and cold and wet.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.510
That’s an hour walk, that walk in the night.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.069
It’s an hour and a half back to the house.

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:01.610
It gives you a lot of time to think.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:09.170
Film Courage: Have we learned anything about
this incident in terms of how we view a scandal?

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.749
Jason Clarke: Have we as a society?

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:14.900
No…no I don’t think so, no.

